WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:09.327
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:17.028
Unit six: Listening: Listen and Choose a Response. Today you will choose the best reply to a short spoken sentence.

4
00:00:17.328 --> 00:00:19.771
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.838
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:25.835
Response.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.593
Response means an answer to what someone says.

8
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:31.684
Choose the best response.

9
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:35.168
Your turn. Say: Response.

10
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.185
Response.

11
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.090
Appropriate.

12
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:44.489
Appropriate means right for the situation.

13
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:47.071
Choose the appropriate reply.

14
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:50.243
Your turn. Say: Appropriate.

15
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.206
Appropriate.

16
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:55.823
Polite.

17
00:00:56.817 --> 00:00:58.944
Polite means showing good manners.

18
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.526
A polite request is better.

19
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.467
Your turn. Say: Polite.

20
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.172
Polite.

21
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:09.848
Request.

22
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.674
Request means asking someone to do something.

23
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:15.923
He made a request for help.

24
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:19.262
Your turn. Say: Request.

25
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.113
Request.

26
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:24.700
Offer.

27
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:28.308
Offer means saying you will give or do something.

28
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:30.533
She made an offer to help.

29
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:33.293
Your turn. Say: Offer.

30
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:37.111
Offer.

31
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:38.851
Suggestion.

32
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.302
Suggestion means an idea about what to do.

33
00:01:42.917 --> 00:01:44.707
Her suggestion was a good one.

34
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:47.806
Your turn. Say: Suggestion.

35
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:51.656
Suggestion.

36
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:53.336
Literal.

37
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.000
Literal means exactly what the words say.

38
00:01:57.583 --> 00:01:59.409
Do not choose a literal meaning.

39
00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:02.274
Your turn. Say: Literal.

40
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:06.042
Literal.

41
00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:07.842
Intention.

42
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.604
Intention means what a speaker wants to do or say.

43
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:14.251
Understand the speaker's intention.

44
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.398
Your turn. Say: Intention.

45
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:21.327
Intention.

46
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:25.912
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.889
Visal, what is this Listening task?

48
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:42.702
You hear one short sentence, such as a question or a request, and choose the best reply from four written options. The audio plays once, so you must listen carefully.

49
00:02:43.817 --> 00:02:45.596
What makes an answer correct?

50
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:57.172
The correct reply fits the speaker's intention and sounds natural. A wrong option may repeat words from the audio but not answer the question.

51
00:02:58.283 --> 00:02:59.668
How do I prepare?

52
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:12.991
Learn common functions such as requests, offers, suggestions and apologies, and practise natural replies. Do not choose an option only because it repeats a word you heard.

53
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:15.843
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:20.877
Grammar focus: Strategy: intention first, then the reply.

55
00:03:21.177 --> 00:03:22.387
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:23.083 --> 00:03:26.934
Could you lend me a pen? A good reply is 'Sure, here you are.'

57
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.533
An option that repeats 'pen' may be a trap if it does not answer.

58
00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:39.891
Decide what the speaker wants to do, predict a natural reply, and choose the option that fits. Avoid options that only repeat words.

59
00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.823
Be careful! Don't say: I choose the option with the same word.

60
00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:49.451
Say: I choose the option that answers.

61
00:03:49.751 --> 00:03:51.414
Do not word-match.

62
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.678
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:56.944
Choosing a Reply.

64
00:03:57.244 --> 00:04:00.021
A student hears: Would you mind closing the window?

65
00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:03.985
She predicts that a polite answer will agree or explain.

66
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.824
One option says: The window is very big.

67
00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:11.959
Another option says: Sure, I will close it now.

68
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:15.698
She chooses the reply that matches the request.

69
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.558
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:21.417 --> 00:04:23.741
In this task the audio plays blank.

71
00:04:24.041 --> 00:04:27.846
A, once. B, five times. C, never.

72
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.458
The answer is A: once.

73
00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:37.104
The correct reply fits the speaker's blank.

74
00:04:37.404 --> 00:04:41.115
A, age. B, intention. C, handwriting.

75
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.922
The answer is B: intention.

76
00:04:48.083 --> 00:04:50.863
Would you mind closing the window? is a blank.

77
00:04:51.163 --> 00:04:54.898
A, offer. B, apology. C, request.

78
00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:00.294
The answer is C: request.

79
00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.007
A word-matching option is often blank.

80
00:05:04.307 --> 00:05:08.065
A, a trap. B, always right. C, a map.

81
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.472
The answer is A: a trap.

82
00:05:14.617 --> 00:05:16.628
Which reply fits the request?

83
00:05:16.928 --> 00:05:22.079
A, I like windows.. B, Sure, I will close it now.. C, The window is big..

84
00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:28.794
The answer is B: Sure, I will close it now..

85
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.535
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:34.383 --> 00:05:36.429
How do you choose the best response?

87
00:05:45.217 --> 00:05:46.636
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:46.936 --> 00:06:00.783
I listen for what the speaker wants, such as a request, an offer or a suggestion. Then I predict a natural reply before I read the options. I avoid options that only repeat words from the audio.

89
00:06:01.083 --> 00:06:02.896
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:03.783 --> 00:06:09.524
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: intention first, then the reply.

91
00:06:09.824 --> 00:06:14.787
Today's value is practical wisdom. Judge ideas by how well they work in real life.

92
00:06:15.087 --> 00:06:17.391
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

93
00:06:17.691 --> 00:06:20.726
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

